---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href, CONTACT_LINKS } from '../lib/site';

/*
 * SOURCE TRACE (Site build 3, second back-propagation round, 2026-08-26)
 *
 * Body prose: `notes/career-narrative-draft.md` §2 at v5, which is the
 * LinkedIn About v9 (`linkedin-rewrite-draft.md` §24, the final round-12
 * form) carried onto this surface, plus the one paragraph ruled to the site
 * About only: the unfinished charge (§21 routing). Operator ruling: "this
 * whole thing should be much closer to the linkedin final version than the
 * original." The change record is §10 of the career-narrative file.
 *
 * Consequences of the v9 form, all recorded in §10:
 *   - No layoff telling on this surface; "before my tenure ended" is the
 *     only departure signal, matching v9's no-status-disclosure posture.
 *   - The identity taxonomy lives in the page lede only and is not repeated
 *     in the body (§24 dependency: if the lede changes, revisit the opener).
 *   - SVP title and the about-ninety figure live on the Home provenance
 *     line, the site's equivalent of the Experience entries.
 *   - The field guide is framed by its own page and the Home feature, not
 *     here.
 *
 * Site-only additions: the Standards pointer sentence after the confession,
 * the Writing pointer in the close, and the contact sentence that renders
 * only once CONTACT_LINKS has entries.
 *
 * Voice: zero em-dashes.
 */
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="The career behind the question: Micron, Tableau, Comscore, and why this work has the shape it does."
  ogSlug="about"
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Engineering executive and practicing architect. I work out what changes
      in a software organization when AI becomes part of the system.
    </p>
    <hr class="page-head__rule" />
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I've spent my career at the intersection of engineering organizations
      and the systems they build: Micron, then Tableau, then Comscore, where
      I ran enterprise architecture and technical strategy for a technology
      org of 200-plus.
    </p>

    <p>
      AI is making implementation cheap. I think that moves engineering's
      real constraints to choosing what to build, verifying what got built,
      and owning the result. I'm spending the next six months, through
      February 2027, testing that against real cases in public.
    </p>

    <p>
      I came to this as the skeptic. Through 2025 the wins I saw weren't
      systematic at our scale. Then one of my managers instrumented a
      multi-petabyte pipeline I'd been told was effectively unobservable, in
      weeks, and first-commit time in legacy repos fell from months to weeks.
      Not proof; enough to dig.
    </p>

    <p>
      The research process I built has one job: break my conclusions. The
      first thing it caught was me. I had commissioned my own conclusion.
      What it produces and where the models sit are on{' '}
      <a href={href('/standards')}>Standards</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      My last charge was the course from a legacy data estate to an
      architecture that could carry the business into its next decade. I
      believed a data mesh could do it if we solved the governance problem. We
      solved neither before my tenure ended. The diagnosis is still untested,
      and that stings. It is also the reason this work exists in the shape it
      does: I got to observe and teach, and I did not get to enact and
      measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      I publish the evidence, the gaps, and, when a decision has to be made
      anyway, the call I'd make. The pieces are on{' '}
      <a href={href('/writing')}>Writing</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you've watched AI change how your teams deliver, or fail to, I want
      the specifics: what moved, what broke, and what refused to get cheaper.
      {
        CONTACT_LINKS.length > 0 && (
          <>
            {' '}The fastest way to reach me is{' '}
            {CONTACT_LINKS.map((link, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && ' or '}
                <a href={link.href} rel="me noopener">
                  {link.label}
                </a>
              </>
            ))}
            .
          </>
        )
      }
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
